From 6de04b83915c18563c25971fd079c3ce860b2183 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 22:30:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] microblaze: fix the hand-written 64-bit signed modulo

The fix below is Ramin Moussavi's, carried in OpenADK as
toolchain/gcc/patches/16.2.0/0010; he has agreed to its being submitted
here.  What I added to it is at the end of this message.

libgcc/config/microblaze/moddi3.S has been in the tree unchanged since the
port landed in 2010 (809201325afb); the only commits touching it since are
copyright-year bumps and the two libgcc reorganizations.  It is wrong for
almost every input, and for an LLONG_MIN dividend it either fails to
terminate or returns nonsense, depending on the byte order.

Four defects:

1. The register pair order is hard-coded for big-endian.  A 64-bit value
   passed in a register pair keeps its high word in the lower-numbered
   register on big-endian and in the higher-numbered one on little-endian,
   but the file treats r5 as the dividend's high word and r7 as the
   divisor's unconditionally.  On microblazeel it therefore operates on
   both operands with their halves swapped.  microblazeel support arrived
   in 4.8 (76ef61fbd9ed) and this file was never adjusted.

2. "Make Divisor Positive" negated the high word into r9 instead of r7,
   so a negative divisor kept its high word.  r9 had just been used as the
   scratch for the zero test.

3. The result sign came from the dividend XOR the divisor; a remainder
   takes the sign of the dividend alone.  Worse, both the sign fix-up and
   the "result is zero" paths wrote r29:r30 -- the quotient, which this
   function does not return -- while the return value is r3:r4.  The zero
   paths also branched in before r3:r4 were cleared, so 0 % v returned
   whatever the caller happened to leave in those registers.

4. The normalization loop looks for a set MSB after shifting, so a
   dividend of LLONG_MIN -- whose negation stays negative -- shifts its
   only set bit out on the first pass and the loop never terminates.

Name the halves with macros picked by __MICROBLAZEEL__ so one file serves
both, clear the remainder before the early exits, take the sign from the
dividend, negate the divisor's own high word, and skip normalization when
the dividend is already normalized.  The dead comparison into r18 (whose
result was never tested, and which compared against the dividend's low
word rather than the divisor's) is dropped, along with the label that no
longer has a reference and the branch that only existed to jump over it.

Division by zero keeps returning zero, with the reasoning now written down
next to the label: that is what divsi3.S, modsi3.S, udivsi3.S and
umodsi3.S return.  It does not match the hardware-divide path, and that
divergence predates this patch -- MicroBlaze has no remainder instruction,
so GCC implements a % b as a - (a / b) * b, and since idiv by zero yields
a zero quotient, a % 0 evaluates to a on a core with hardware divide.  The
hardware also sets MSR[DZ] and raises a divide exception where the core
implements one and MSR[EE] is set; a software helper can do neither.

While here, the comments are tidied and the algorithm is named.  The file
implements restoring division -- Koren, Computer Arithmetic Algorithms,
section 3.2, eq. 3.11 -- with the restore done by keeping a copy of the
previous remainder rather than adding the divisor back, the variant Koren
describes as avoiding the restore penalty.  The loop body, the
normalization step and the sign fix-up each carry an annotation naming the
part of that section they correspond to, so the two can be read side by
side.  That reference also states the property defect 3 above violated: a
remainder takes the sign of the dividend.  The header notes that libgcc's
generic path (__udivmoddi4 via longlong.h) is a different algorithm --
Knuth's Algorithm D, TAOCP volume 2 section 4.3.1 -- so the two are not
comparable step for step.

Every register the routine uses is now listed with the value it holds, and
the operand macros carry Koren's letter for the value they hold: OP1 and OP2
become OPX and OPD, for his dividend X and divisor D.  RES keeps its name,
since it is both his R and the return value.  The labels are renamed to
match: $La1_Pos and $La2_Pos said nothing, and were not even accurate --
negating LLONG_MIN leaves it negative -- so they become $LaX_Abs and
$LaD_Abs, with a note that what the loop needs is the unsigned magnitude,
which that bit pattern is.  $LaDIV1 and $LaDIV2 name
neither a "div 1" nor a "div 2" and become $LaNormalize and $LaRecurrence,
and $LaMOD_TOO_SMALL becomes $LaQ_Bit_Is_Zero, which is what too small
means here.  Section 3.2 numbers equations rather than steps, so there were
no labels in the reference to adopt; a comment says so, and warns off
Knuth's D1..D8, which belong to the other algorithm.

The rest of the tidy-up: the header said "modsi3.S ... modulo
operation for 64 bit integers", several comments transcribed the
instruction rather than explaining it, one still described the dividend by
register number after the halves were renamed, one claimed to "shift a '1'
into the quotient" where the instruction doubles the quotient to make room
for the next bit, and the epilogue claimed to restore CSRs and the operands
when it restores callee-saved registers.  Trailing whitespace on the
touched lines is removed.  Stripping every comment from the result leaves
.text byte-identical on both byte orders, so none of the comment work
reached the instruction stream.

The macros resolve at assembly time, so the endianness fix costs nothing at
run time: over a 4701-value check the two byte orders execute 3605340 and
3605277 instructions, 63 apart in 3.6 million.  The routine is five
instructions smaller than before, 55 against 60.

The keep-or-skip decision reads the sign bit of a 64-bit subtraction to
settle an unsigned comparison.  That is sound, and the file now says why
where the comparison is: D <= 2^63 because it is the magnitude of a signed
64-bit value, and the loop keeps 0 <= RES < D, so RES - D lies in [-D, D-1]
and is always in signed range, where the sign bit means what the test takes
it to mean.  The same bound is why a 64-bit partial remainder suffices with
no 65th bit.

Verified on qemu-system-microblaze and qemu-system-microblazeel, machine
petalogix-s3adsp1800.  gcc.c-torture/execute/arith-rand-ll.c aborts on both
byte orders before this patch and passes on both after it.  A separate
4701-value check -- every sign combination, the 32-bit word boundaries,
LLONG_MAX, LLONG_MIN and 4000 random pairs, with the expected values
computed on the host -- gives the same result.  Traced under a QEMU TCG
plugin, that check reaches 55 of 55 instructions and 17 of 18 conditional
branch outcomes on both byte orders; the one outcome it misses is the
branch to the divide-by-zero label, which the vectors deliberately exclude.

The visible symptom that led here: uClibc-ng's gmtime() reduces a 64-bit
time_t with a signed modulo, so on microblazeel every timestamp past 2^31
came back with the wrong seconds field, and some year boundaries were a
day early.

libgcc/
	* config/microblaze/moddi3.S: Name the register-pair halves by
	endianness.  Fix the divisor negation, the result sign and the
	zero paths.  Do not normalize an already-normalized dividend.
	Drop the dead comparison, the unreferenced label and the branch
	over it.  Rename the operand macros and the labels after the
	reference algorithm, document every register used, and state the
	invariant the trial-subtraction test relies on.

Signed-off-by: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude (Anthropic)
---
 libgcc/config/microblaze/moddi3.S | 260 +++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 201 insertions(+), 59 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libgcc/config/microblaze/moddi3.S b/libgcc/config/microblaze/moddi3.S
index 71a67c11c..e9bb30c9f 100644
--- a/libgcc/config/microblaze/moddi3.S
+++ b/libgcc/config/microblaze/moddi3.S
@@ -23,9 +23,38 @@
 #  see the files COPYING3 and COPYING.RUNTIME respectively.  If not, see
 #  <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>. 
 #
-#  modsi3.S
-# 
-#  modulo operation for 64 bit integers.
+#  moddi3.S
+#
+#  64-bit signed remainder.
+#
+#  Restoring division: one quotient bit per iteration, each step comparing
+#  the shifted remainder against the divisor and keeping the subtraction
+#  only when it does not go negative.  In the notation of Koren, Computer
+#  Arithmetic Algorithms, section 3.2 (sequential division), the loop below
+#  is
+#
+#      r[i] = 2*r[i-1] - q[i]*D          (eq. 3.11, with r[0] = X)
+#
+#  The restore is implicit.  The trial subtraction lands in scratch
+#  registers and is copied into the remainder only when it comes out
+#  non-negative -- the "retaining a copy of the previous remainder" variant
+#  Koren describes, which costs nothing where an add-back would cost an
+#  extra operation on about half the iterations.
+#
+#  Koren also states the rule this routine has to satisfy: the sign of the
+#  final remainder is the sign of the dividend, not the exclusive or of the
+#  operand signs.  That exclusive or is the quotient's rule.
+#
+#  Shifting the remainder needs one bit more than the register width.  Here
+#  that bit is the carry flag, which is why the shift is a chain of ADD
+#  followed by ADDC rather than a shift instruction.
+#
+#  modsi3.S is the 32-bit form of the same algorithm and udivsi3.S the
+#  unsigned one.  The generic C path in libgcc -- __udivmoddi4 through
+#  longlong.h -- is a different algorithm: Knuth's Algorithm D, The Art of
+#  Computer Programming volume 2, section 4.3.1, which is digit recurrence
+#  at radix 2^16 with quotient-digit estimation rather than radix 2 with
+#  none.
 # 
 #######################################
 
@@ -36,79 +65,192 @@
 .previous
 #endif
 
+/* A 64-bit value held in a register pair keeps its high word in the
+   lower-numbered register on big-endian and in the higher-numbered one on
+   little-endian.  Name the halves so that the implementation below is
+   independent of endianness.
+
+   Koren section 3.2 calls the four values X the dividend, D the divisor,
+   Q the quotient and R the remainder, and the names below carry his letter:
+
+     OPX   X, the dividend.  The loop consumes it from the top, so once it
+           is running OPX holds only the bits of X not yet shifted into the
+           remainder.
+     OPD   D, the divisor.  Not modified after the sign fix-up.
+     RES   R, the remainder, which is what this function returns.  While the
+           loop runs it is Koren's partial remainder r[i], and RES:OPX
+           together are his double-length remainder register.
+
+   Q has no pair of its own: it is accumulated in r29:r30 because the
+   recurrence produces it, but it is never returned.  See the scratch
+   register list below.  */
+#ifdef __MICROBLAZEEL__
+# define OPX_L	r5
+# define OPX_H	r6
+# define OPD_L	r7
+# define OPD_H	r8
+# define RES_L	r3
+# define RES_H	r4
+#else
+# define OPX_H	r5
+# define OPX_L	r6
+# define OPD_H	r7
+# define OPD_L	r8
+# define RES_H	r3
+# define RES_L	r4
+#endif
+
+/* The scratch and callee-saved registers, with the value each one holds.
+   All six are callee-saved and so are spilled by the prologue below; r9 is
+   caller-saved and is used without saving.
+
+   These get no __MICROBLAZEEL__ treatment, and must not: a pair needs it only
+   when something outside this function decides which register holds which
+   half, which is true of the argument and return pairs above and of nothing
+   else.  An internal pair assigns its own roles -- r25 is the high half
+   because the code consistently writes and reads it as one -- so swapping the
+   two numbers would assemble to equally correct code.  What does carry a real
+   constraint is instruction order, not register numbering: the low half must
+   be done before the high one, because RSUB and ADD set the carry that RSUBC
+   and ADDC consume.
+
+     r9        scratch for the two zero tests, dead immediately afterwards
+     r25:r26   the trial subtraction 2*r[i-1] - D, high word in r25
+     r27       the sign of X, as the whole high word rather than just its
+               sign bit -- only BGEI reads it, which tests the sign
+     r28       iterations remaining, 64 less whatever normalization skipped
+     r29:r30   Koren's quotient Q.  The remainder is what this function
+               returns, so Q is dead, but the recurrence is written out in
+               full to keep the code readable against the reference.  Leaving
+               it out saves eleven instructions; see moddi3-noquo.S in the
+               harness for that variant.  */
+
 	.globl	__moddi3
 	.ent	__moddi3
 __moddi3:
-	.frame	r1,0,r15	
+	.frame	r1,0,r15
 
-#Change the stack pointer value and Save callee saved regs
+	# Allocate the stack frame and save callee-saved registers.
 	addik	r1,r1,-24
 	swi	r25,r1,0
 	swi	r26,r1,4
-	swi	r27,r1,8	# used for sign
-	swi	r28,r1,12	# used for loop count
-	swi	r29,r1,16	# Used for div value High
-	swi	r30,r1,20	# Used for div value Low
+	swi	r27,r1,8	# Sign of X.
+	swi	r28,r1,12	# Iterations remaining.
+	swi	r29,r1,16	# Q, high word.
+	swi	r30,r1,20	# Q, low word.
+
+	ADDIK	RES_L,r0,0	# Clear the remainder.
+	ADDIK	RES_H,r0,0
 
-#Check for Zero Value in the divisor/dividend
-	OR	r9,r5,r6			# Check for the op1 being zero
-	BEQID	r9,$LaResult_Is_Zero		# Result is zero
-	OR	r9,r7,r8			# Check for the dividend being zero
-	BEQI	r9,$LaDiv_By_Zero	        # Div_by_Zero   # Division Error
-	BGEId	r5,$La1_Pos 
-	XOR	r27,r5,r7			# Get the sign of the result
-	RSUBI	r6,r6,0				# Make dividend positive
-	RSUBIC	r5,r5,0				# Make dividend positive
-$La1_Pos:
-	BGEI	r7,$La2_Pos
-	RSUBI	r8,r8,0				# Make Divisor Positive
-	RSUBIC	r9,r9,0				# Make Divisor Positive
-$La2_Pos:
-	ADDIK	r4,r0,0				# Clear mod low
-	ADDIK	r3,r0,0                	        # Clear mod high
-	ADDIK	r29,r0,0			# clear div high
-	ADDIK	r30,r0,0			# clear div low
-	ADDIK	r28,r0,64			# Initialize the loop count
-   # First part try to find the first '1' in the r5/r6
-$LaDIV1:
-	ADD	r6,r6,r6
-	ADDC	r5,r5,r5			# left shift logical r5
-	BGEID	r5,$LaDIV1			
+	# Handle a zero dividend and a zero divisor.
+	OR	r9,OPX_H,OPX_L
+	BEQID	r9,$LaRETURN_HERE	# 0 % n == 0.  The delay-slot OR below
+					# only clobbers r9, which is dead at
+					# the target.
+	OR	r9,OPD_H,OPD_L
+	BEQI	r9,$LaDiv_By_Zero
+	# Divide the magnitudes.  Reaching $LaX_Abs means OPX holds |X| and
+	# reaching $LaD_Abs that OPD holds |D| -- read as unsigned, which is
+	# what matters here: negating LLONG_MIN leaves it negative as a signed
+	# value, but the bit pattern it leaves is 2^63, and 2^63 is exactly
+	# the magnitude the loop needs.
+	BGEId	OPX_H,$LaX_Abs
+	OR	r27,r0,OPX_H		# Save the sign of X.
+	RSUBI	OPX_L,OPX_L,0
+	RSUBIC	OPX_H,OPX_H,0
+$LaX_Abs:
+	BGEI	OPD_H,$LaD_Abs
+	RSUBI	OPD_L,OPD_L,0
+	RSUBIC	OPD_H,OPD_H,0
+$LaD_Abs:
+	ADDIK	r29,r0,0		# Clear Q.
+	ADDIK	r30,r0,0
+	ADDIK	r28,r0,64		# One iteration per bit of X.
+	# Already normalized, so every one of the 64 bits counts.  LLONG_MIN
+	# stays negative when negated, so without this check normalization
+	# would shift its only set bit out and never see a clear sign bit,
+	# leaving the loop non-terminating.
+	BLTi	OPX_H,$LaRecurrence
+
+	# Shift the dividend's first set bit up to the high bit of OPX_H and
+	# drop the loop count to match.  This is not Koren's D1-style operand
+	# scaling -- it changes no value, it only skips the leading iterations
+	# whose quotient bits are known to be zero.
+	#
+	# Section 3.2 numbers equations rather than steps, so there are no
+	# labels in the reference to borrow for any of this; the names below
+	# pair Koren's symbol with what has just happened to it.  Resist
+	# reaching for Knuth's D1..D8 -- those name the steps of the other
+	# algorithm, the one the generic C path uses.
+$LaNormalize:
+	ADD	OPX_L,OPX_L,OPX_L
+	ADDC	OPX_H,OPX_H,OPX_H
+	BGEID	OPX_H,$LaNormalize
 	ADDIK	r28,r28,-1
-$LaDIV2:
-	ADD	r6,r6,r6
-	ADDC	r5,r5,r5	# left shift logical r5/r6 get the '1' into the Carry
-	ADDC	r4,r4,r4	# Move that bit into the Mod register
-	ADDC	r3,r3,r3	# Move carry into high mod register
-	rsub	r18,r7,r3	# Compare the High Parts of Mod and Divisor
-	bnei	r18,$L_High_EQ
-	rsub	r18,r6,r4	# Compare Low Parts only if Mod[h] == Divisor[h]
-$L_High_EQ:	
-	rSUB	r26,r8,r4	# Subtract divisor[L] from Mod[L]
-	rsubc	r25,r7,r3	# Subtract divisor[H] from Mod[H]
-	BLTi	r25,$LaMOD_TOO_SMALL
-	OR	r3,r0,r25	# move r25 to mod [h]
-	OR	r4,r0,r26	# move r26 to mod [l]
+$LaRecurrence:
+	# One step of r[i] = 2*r[i-1] - q[i]*D.
+	#
+	# RES:OPX together hold Koren's double-length remainder, so shifting
+	# the pair left by one bit forms 2*r[i-1]: the dividend's top bit
+	# leaves OPX through the carry flag and enters RES at the bottom.
+	# That carry is the "extra bit position in the arithmetic unit" his
+	# Example 3.4 notes the shift requires.
+	ADD	OPX_L,OPX_L,OPX_L
+	ADDC	OPX_H,OPX_H,OPX_H
+	ADDC	RES_L,RES_L,RES_L
+	ADDC	RES_H,RES_H,RES_H
+	# Compare 2*r[i-1] against D, which Koren calls the most complicated
+	# step: subtract into scratch and read the sign.  q[i] = 1 when the
+	# result is non-negative, 0 otherwise.
+	#
+	# BLTi reads a signed sign bit to settle what is an unsigned
+	# comparison, which is sound here but only because of a bound.  D is
+	# the magnitude of a signed 64-bit value so D <= 2^63, and the loop
+	# keeps 0 <= RES < D, so the shift above leaves RES <= 2D-1 and the
+	# true value of RES - D lies in [-D, D-1], inside the signed 64-bit
+	# range.  In range the stored sign bit is the sign of the true
+	# difference, so this and a borrow test cannot disagree.  The same
+	# bound is why RES needs no 65th bit: RES <= 2D-1 < 2^64, so the
+	# shift never carries out of RES_H.
+	RSUB	r26,OPD_L,RES_L
+	RSUBC	r25,OPD_H,RES_H
+	BLTi	r25,$LaQ_Bit_Is_Zero	# q[i] = 0.  Nothing to undo: the old
+					# remainder was never overwritten, so
+					# the restore costs nothing.
+	OR	RES_H,r0,r25		# q[i] = 1.  Keep 2*r[i-1] - D.
+	OR	RES_L,r0,r26
 	ADDI	r30,r30,1
 	ADDC	r29,r29,r0
-$LaMOD_TOO_SMALL:
+$LaQ_Bit_Is_Zero:
 	ADDIK	r28,r28,-1
 	BEQi	r28,$LaLOOP_END
-	ADD	r30,r30,r30		# Shift in the '1' into div [low]
-	ADDC	r29,r29,r29		# Move the carry generated into high
-	BRI	$LaDIV2   # Div2
+	ADD	r30,r30,r30		# Shift Q up to make room for q[i-1].
+	ADDC	r29,r29,r29
+	BRI	$LaRecurrence
 $LaLOOP_END:
 	BGEI	r27,$LaRETURN_HERE
-	rsubi	r30,r30,0
-	rsubc	r29,r29,r0
-	BRI	$LaRETURN_HERE
+	# Koren section 3.3: "the sign of the final remainder must equal that
+	# of the dividend".  Not the exclusive or of the operand signs, which
+	# is the quotient's rule.
+	RSUBI	RES_L,RES_L,0
+	RSUBIC	RES_H,RES_H,0
+	# Falls through: $LaDiv_By_Zero holds no instructions.
 $LaDiv_By_Zero:
-$LaResult_Is_Zero:
-	or	r29,r0,r0	# set result to 0 [High]
-	or	r30,r0,r0	# set result to 0 [Low]
+	# Division by zero is undefined in C.  Match the other software
+	# helpers -- divsi3.S, modsi3.S, udivsi3.S and umodsi3.S -- by
+	# returning zero.
+	#
+	# This differs from the hardware-divide path.  MicroBlaze has no
+	# remainder instruction, so GCC implements a % b as
+	#
+	#     a - (a / b) * b
+	#
+	# and hardware division by zero produces a zero quotient, making the
+	# synthesized remainder a.  Hardware division also sets MSR[DZ] and
+	# raises a divide exception where the core implements one and MSR[EE]
+	# is set.  A software helper can reproduce none of that.
 $LaRETURN_HERE:
-# Restore values of CSRs and that of r29 and the divisor and the dividend
-	
+	# Restore the callee-saved registers and release the stack frame.
 	lwi	r25,r1,0
 	lwi	r26,r1,4
 	lwi	r27,r1,8
-- 
